# XCU tabs
# Copyright (c) 2026 Nicholas Martin. All rights reserved.
#
# The tests run tabs on a private pseudo-terminal (core/pcpty.c). The escape
# sequence is unspecified, so they compare outputs for the equivalences the
# page states, and check the success-or-diagnostic rule.

assertion  XCU.tabs.001
anchor     utilities/tabs.html#DESCRIPTION
anchor     utilities/tabs.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/tabs.html#OPERANDS
anchor     utilities/tabs.html#STDOUT
class      untestable
says       The bytes written for a given tab list are the terminal's sequence
           that clears and sets the stops.
reason     The sequence is unspecified; only the equivalences between
           invocations can be observed.

assertion  XCU.tabs.002
anchor     utilities/tabs.html#EXIT STATUS
anchor     utilities/tabs.html#STDERR
class      required
since      1992
says       tabs with a terminal type either succeeds or exits nonzero with a
           diagnostic; it never fails silently.
test       t/002-run.sh

assertion  XCU.tabs.003
anchor     utilities/tabs.html#OPTIONS
class      required
since      1992
says       tabs with no operand has the same effect as tabs -8.
test       t/003-default.sh

assertion  XCU.tabs.004
anchor     utilities/tabs.html#OPERANDS
class      required
since      1992
says       A tab list given with commas, blanks or plus-signed increments means
           the same stops, so the output is identical.
test       t/004-list-forms.sh

assertion  XCU.tabs.005
anchor     utilities/tabs.html#OPTIONS
class      required
since      1992
says       The terminal type from -T takes precedence over TERM.
test       t/005-t-overrides.sh

assertion  XCU.tabs.006
anchor     utilities/tabs.html#OPTIONS
anchor     utilities/tabs.html#EXIT STATUS
class      required
since      1992
says       An unknown option is an error with a nonzero status.
test       t/006-bad-option.sh

assertion  XCU.tabs.007
anchor     utilities/tabs.html#OPTIONS
class      conditional
when       XSI
since      1992
says       Each of -a, -a2, -c, -c2, -c3, -f, -p, -s and -u gives the same effect
           as its listed tab stops.
test       t/007-xsi-presets.sh
